{"name":"Sports Blue","hex":"#399bb4","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"sports","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["plural of sport"]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Synonym of sport (“the class of physical activies; athletics”)."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/sports"},{"term":"blue","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a blue hue.","Depressed, melancholic, sad."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The colour of the clear sky or the deep sea; the colour midway between green and violet in the visible spectrum and one of the primary additive colours.","Anything coloured blue, especially to distinguish it from similar objects differing only in colour."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/blue"}],"entries":[{"title":"Sport","blocks":[{"text":"A sport is a physical activity or game, often competitive and organised, that maintains or improves physical ability and skills. Sport may provide enjoyment to participants and entertainment to spectators. The number of participants in a particular sport can vary from hundreds of people to a single individual."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/92/Youth-soccer-indiana.jpg/330px-Youth-soccer-indiana.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sport","wikidata":"Q349"},{"title":"Blue","blocks":[{"text":"Blue is one of the three primary colours in the RGB (additive) colour model, as well as in the RYB colour model. It lies between violet and cyan on the spectrum of visible light. The term blue generally describes colours perceived by humans observing light with a dominant wavelength between approximately 450 and 495 nanometres. The clear daytime sky and the deep sea appear blue because of an optical effect known as Rayleigh scattering. An optical effect called the Tyndall effect explains blue eyes. Distant objects appear more blue because of another optical effect called aerial perspective."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5a/Earth-in-true-color-himawari-09.jpg/330px-Earth-in-true-color-himawari-09.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Blue","wikidata":"Q1088"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/ad/London_Marathon_2005_at_Blackfriars.jpg/500px-London_Marathon_2005_at_Blackfriars.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/ad/London_Marathon_2005_at_Blackfriars.jpg/1280px-London_Marathon_2005_at_Blackfriars.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/ad/London_Marathon_2005_at_Blackfriars.jpg/500px-London_Marathon_2005_at_Blackfriars.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"The 2005 London Marathon: running races, in their various specialties, represent the oldest and most traditional form of sport.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ALondon_Marathon_2005_at_Blackfriars.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/30/ChessStartingPosition.jpg/500px-ChessStartingPosition.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/30/ChessStartingPosition.jpg/1280px-ChessStartingPosition.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/30/ChessStartingPosition.jpg/500px-ChessStartingPosition.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"The International Olympic Committee recognises some board games as sports, including chess.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AChessStartingPosition.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimed